{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 470,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s so crazy that I just happen to be in\none of the rare places in our universe where"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6140,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I don’t instantly asphyxiate or freeze or\nvaporize or dehydrate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11170,
    "dDurationMs": 1510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "... Just lucky I guess."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12680,
    "dDurationMs": 5710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually, it turns out that our very privileged\nperspective on the universe from Earth’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18390,
    "dDurationMs": 4519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "comfortable biosphere may tell us a lot about our reality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22909,
    "dDurationMs": 2931,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And perhaps resolves the Fermi Paradox."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30980,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It shouldn’t be surprising that we live\non a planet that can support our existence,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35850,
    "dDurationMs": 2830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a universe that can produce such planets."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38680,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The anthropic principle tells us that we shouldn’t\nexpect to find ourselves in some random corner"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43680,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the multiverse - there’s an observer\nbias."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47750,
    "dDurationMs": 5860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In upcoming episodes we’ll be exploring\nthis principle and its two main versions - the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53610,
    "dDurationMs": 3410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strong and the weak anthropic principles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57020,
    "dDurationMs": 4559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The strong anthropic principle tells us that\nan observed universe must be able to produce"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61579,
    "dDurationMs": 5991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observers - and we’ll get to the implications\nof that soon - including the contentious idea"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67570,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that this predicts the existence of universes\nbeyond our own."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71010,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But today we’re going to focus on the weak\nanthropic principle, although it’s anything"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75850,
    "dDurationMs": 1220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but weak."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77070,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It says that we must find ourselves in a part\nof the universe capable of supporting us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82990,
    "dDurationMs": 6450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, in a planetary biosphere rather\nthan floating in the void between the galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89440,
    "dDurationMs": 6430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This may seems tautological, but accounting\nfor this observer selection bias is important"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95870,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to understanding why the universe looks the\nway it does from our perspective."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101020,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the weak anthropic principle is much more\nuseful than that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104500,
    "dDurationMs": 6010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When combined with the apparent absence of\nalien civilizations, it may tell us that intelligent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110510,
    "dDurationMs": 3830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "life is incredibly rare in our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114340,
    "dDurationMs": 5139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To get to this, let's think about what it\nmeans to be an intelligent observer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119479,
    "dDurationMs": 4621,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Your mental experience of thinking about these\nquestions ... exists."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124100,
    "dDurationMs": 1780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's happening right now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125880,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Your type of mental experience - your type\nof being - could be incredibly rare - even"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130790,
    "dDurationMs": 4779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unique - and only be possible in very unusual\nenvironments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135569,
    "dDurationMs": 3181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But you're having that experience - you ARE\nthat experience."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138750,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So no matter how rare these you-supporting\nenvironments really are - by definition you’re"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145310,
    "dDurationMs": 1340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146650,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The weak anthropic principle places no limit\non how rare those you-supporting environments"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152620,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153620,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, if there’s only one life-bearing\nplanet in the galaxy, or in the universe, you’re"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159390,
    "dDurationMs": 1420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going to be on it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160810,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The rare earth hypothesis posits exactly\nthis - that a range of factors made Earth"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166900,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exceptionally unusual and uniquely able to\nproduce intelligent life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171860,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This hypothesis was inspired by some striking\nobservations about our home planet, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176980,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’ll get to - but also by one other piece\nof evidence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180360,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or, rather, a lack thereof - the fact that\nwe see no evidence for aliens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185910,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Fermi Paradox notes the apparent contradiction\nbetween the massive abundance of potential"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191370,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "opportunities for technical life to have emerged\nand spread through our galaxy and the apparent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196860,
    "dDurationMs": 2290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lack of galactic civilizations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199150,
    "dDurationMs": 3699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, we’ve talked about the Fermi Paradox before,\nand some potential solutions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202849,
    "dDurationMs": 5811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But let’s consider the possibility that\nit is exactly how it seems - technological"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208660,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "civilizations are exceedingly rare - and maybe\nthat’s because Earth is an exceedingly rare planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215380,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The solution to the Fermi paradox is often\nexpressed in terms of one or more great filters"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220980,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- extremely difficult or unlikely steps in\nthe development from barren planet to visible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226560,
    "dDurationMs": 2110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technological civilization."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228670,
    "dDurationMs": 6650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Such a filter might be after our own stage\nof development - climate change, nuclear obliteration,  whatever"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235320,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- still waiting out there to wipe us out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238020,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the Rare Earth hypothesis is a little\nmore optimistic - it states that planets capable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242780,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of spawning civilizations even at our own\nlevel are very rare."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247950,
    "dDurationMs": 3750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea was named and brought to popular\nattention in the book by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251700,
    "dDurationMs": 3100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Peter Ward and Robert Brownlee in 2000."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254810,
    "dDurationMs": 4739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It highlights a series of remarkable qualities\nof planet Earth that may have been needed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259549,
    "dDurationMs": 2561,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for life and intelligence to arise here."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262110,
    "dDurationMs": 1870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s take a look at them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263980,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually, let’s start by something that\nis NOT rare about the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269260,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth-like planets are common."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271940,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And by Earth-like I mean rocky planets about the\nsize of the Earth in orbit around stars very similar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277600,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the Sun at the right distance to sustain\nliquid water on their surface - in the so-called"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283130,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "habitable or Goldilocks zone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285370,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Kepler mission has revealed there should\nbe 10 billion or so in our galaxy - 40 billion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290780,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if we permit other star types."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293340,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, billions of potential starting points for\nlife in the Milky Way alone, even if we restrict"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298380,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ourselves to boring old carbon-based water-loving\nplanet life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302900,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s billions of planets stewing for billions\nof years - if only one civilization had a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308340,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tiny head start on us then it could have colonized\nthe galaxy by now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312240,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unless Earth has special qualities that mean\ntrue Earth-like planets are much rarer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316890,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s think about what Earth’s has got\nthat seems critical for life and that could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320750,
    "dDurationMs": 1100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be unique."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321850,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we see that even one other planet has some\nlife-critical quality, then we know that that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326400,
    "dDurationMs": 1660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quality could be relatively common."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328060,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if we’ve only ever seen that quality\non Earth then it could be hugely uncommon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333210,
    "dDurationMs": 5549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- and the weak anthropic principle says it’s\nstill not surprising for us to find ourselves"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338760,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on one of the very few planets with that quality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341800,
    "dDurationMs": 3570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll start by comparing planets of our\nsolar system, because our ability to probe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345370,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extra-solar planets is still in its infancy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348430,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Broadly, Earth has two qualities not shared\nby the other rocky planets in our solar system:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354110,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1) it has a very dynamic interior and 2) a\nvery large moon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359660,
    "dDurationMs": 5759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth’s solid iron inner core spins suspended\nin a molten metal outer core, and this motion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365420,
    "dDurationMs": 6100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "generates a powerful magnetic field that protects\nEarth from dangerous space radiation and solar storms."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371520,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Above Earth’s core is a solid mantle which\nstill flows due to its heat."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376330,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This drives plate tectonics on the surface\n- plates of Earth’s crust float around and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381780,
    "dDurationMs": 3919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are periodic drawn back into the mantle, or\nsubducted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385699,
    "dDurationMs": 3531,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This results in shifting connections between\necosystems."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389230,
    "dDurationMs": 6470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that may have been a critical driver of evolution,\npromoting biodiversity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395700,
    "dDurationMs": 5010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The periodic subduction of tectonic plates\nrecycles nutrients from the crust into the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400710,
    "dDurationMs": 3929,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mantle and then back into the atmosphere through\nvolcanic activity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404639,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Without this biogeochemical cycle, many life-critical\nelements may have been lost to the biosphere"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410039,
    "dDurationMs": 1410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "long ago."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411449,
    "dDurationMs": 4961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So Earth’s dynamic interior seems to be\nlife-critical in multiple ways."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416410,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By comparison, Mars is tectonically dead and\nVenus is at best tectonically weak - certainly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421810,
    "dDurationMs": 3259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neither have protective geomagnetic fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425069,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We don’t know whether tectonic activity\nis rare in exoplanets, but it may be."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430229,
    "dDurationMs": 1961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which brings us to the moon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432190,
    "dDurationMs": 5819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth’s moon is ridiculously gigantic - no\nother rocky planet in our system has anything"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438009,
    "dDurationMs": 1190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439199,
    "dDurationMs": 4681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Its size and also its composition and orbit\nsuggest that it formed when a Mars-ish sized"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443880,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planet collided with the Earth right after its\nformation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447910,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The debris thrown up during this collision\nbecame our moon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451810,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, this could be an incredibly rare scenario,\neven galaxy-wide."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456120,
    "dDurationMs": 5199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It may also be that our moon and the event\nthat formed it was critical to the development"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461319,
    "dDurationMs": 1741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463060,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That impact likely gave Earth its rapid rotation\nrate - with short nights essential for photosynthesis,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469360,
    "dDurationMs": 2470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and also its axial tilt."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471830,
    "dDurationMs": 6290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A moderate tilt could be critical if seasons\nare an important driver of evolution."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478120,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Too large a tilt and seasons become too extreme\nfor life to thrive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482150,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth’s tilt seems just right - perhaps\neven rare."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486660,
    "dDurationMs": 6819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That impact may even have kickstarted Earth’s\nextreme tectonic activity by fragmenting Earth’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493479,
    "dDurationMs": 2711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "early crust into moving plates."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496190,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the moon’s later tidal influence may\nalso be an important factor in enhancing ongoing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501150,
    "dDurationMs": 1430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tectonic activity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502580,
    "dDurationMs": 5459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And a final possible result of our weirdly\nlarge moon is that it enabled the first appearance"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508039,
    "dDurationMs": 1121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509160,
    "dDurationMs": 3210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It may have enabled abiogenesis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512370,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One hypothesis for the first formation of\nlife is that it evolved in tidal pools, with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517680,
    "dDurationMs": 5669,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "complex chemicals and eventually proto-cells\nemerging as a primordial soup sloshed in these"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523349,
    "dDurationMs": 2600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pools, baking in the sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525949,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Without a large moon tides are half the size, so fewer tidal pools."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530899,
    "dDurationMs": 4961,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "More recently, alternative hypotheses for\nthe location of abiogenesis have gained favor"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535860,
    "dDurationMs": 3610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- particularly geothermal vents on the ocean\nfloor."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539470,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so Earth is weirdly dynamic and has a\nweirdly giant moon,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544600,
    "dDurationMs": 1410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but there’s more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546010,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our entire planetary system is pretty weird."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549700,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve only figured this out as the Kepler\nmission wrapped up its census of other planetary systems."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554980,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our solar system has a huge range of planet\nproperties - from the tiny rocky Mercury to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560509,
    "dDurationMs": 3091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the gigantic gaseous Jupiter and Saturn."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563600,
    "dDurationMs": 6580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In contrast, the planets of most other systems\ntend to be all around the same size as each other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570180,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and planets as large as Jupiter and\nSaturn are pretty rare - only around 10% of systems."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575680,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And yet Jupiter in particular was probably\npretty important for the development of life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580970,
    "dDurationMs": 5229,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That planet acts like a gigantic gravitational\nvacuum cleaner, absorbing a lot of the debris"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586199,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "left over from the formation of the solar\nsystem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588840,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It no doubt sucked up many comets and asteroids\nwould otherwise have hit the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593400,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we’d had a significantly higher rate\nof mass-extinction-level impacts,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598500,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perhaps evolution would not have progressed so far."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601880,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps life would have been wiped out entirely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604860,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are a few other possible rare Earth factors - we\nmay have an unusually hospitable atmosphere"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610989,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and water content and may have been lucky in avoiding\nvarious cosmic catastrophes like gamma ray"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615949,
    "dDurationMs": 1471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bursts."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617420,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the final thing that may make Earth a cosmic\nrarity is the path taken by evolution."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624200,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps life is extremely common - or at least\nextremely simple life is common."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629120,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps the great filter is one or more extremely\nimprobable steps that happened in the evolutionary"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635440,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "transition from single-cellular life to complex\nlife, or to intelligence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641280,
    "dDurationMs": 4519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just one example of this: the evolution of\nthe eukaryote cell."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645799,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This seems to have been a freak evolutionary\nincident, in which two much simpler cell types"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650959,
    "dDurationMs": 4271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fused - one absorbing the other, perhaps in\na failed attempt at dinner."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655230,
    "dDurationMs": 6259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The absorbed cell became mitochondria, an\nenergy power house that allowed the new chimerical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661489,
    "dDurationMs": 5470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cell to massively increase its complexity,\nultimately leading to the first multicellular"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666959,
    "dDurationMs": 1220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "organisms."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668180,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are many factors that shaped Earth’s\nformation and development - what if"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672040,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Cambrian explosion had never happened, or the asteroid never wiped out the dinosaurs, or an extra"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678180,
    "dDurationMs": 2540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "asteroid wiped out our ancestors?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680720,
    "dDurationMs": 3609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are lots of ways that it seems Earth\ngot lucky."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684329,
    "dDurationMs": 4851,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The question raised by the rare earth hypothesis\nis just how lucky were we?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689180,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many of Earth’s life-critical qualities\nor development steps have not been seen elsewhere,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695480,
    "dDurationMs": 4849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nor do they seem to have happened more than once - at least yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700329,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The weak anthropic principle allows that these\nsingular events were phenomenally unlikely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705929,
    "dDurationMs": 5741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- we simply can’t assign them larger probabilities\nuntil we get more evidence - which ideally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711670,
    "dDurationMs": 2449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means seeing them happen more than once."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714119,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s very possible that a combination of\nextremely unlikely factors means it’s extremely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719759,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rare for planets to spawn intelligence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723529,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Fermi paradox surely has a solution, and\nthat solution may be that the galaxy is as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729499,
    "dDurationMs": 1911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "empty as it looks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731410,
    "dDurationMs": 6489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We find ourselves in the only place we could\nbe: gazing out from our rare earth into the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737899,
    "dDurationMs": 3901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "untamed, unpopulated reaches of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741800,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We skipped comment responses last episode,\nso today we're covering two episodes -"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746000,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "loop quantum gravity and time travel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748780,
    "dDurationMs": 1539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Easy peasy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750319,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A few of you wondered if there's a connection\nbetween the loops of loop quantum gravity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755019,
    "dDurationMs": 2901,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the closed strings of string theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757920,
    "dDurationMs": 1469,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer is not really."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759389,
    "dDurationMs": 4291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The strings of string theory have a somewhat\nphysical interpretation -"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763680,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the fact that they can hold energy and vibrate and exist in space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768400,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the loops of LQG aren't really physical at all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772680,
    "dDurationMs": 6700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which brings us to the most common question\n- what actually ARE the loops of loop quantum gravity?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779380,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, crudely, they're a mathematical way\nto describe the geometry of space - which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784429,
    "dDurationMs": 4921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means they aren't in space, they sort of ARE space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789350,
    "dDurationMs": 6859,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or at least familar 3-D space emerges in an\nabstract way when we think about a network"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796209,
    "dDurationMs": 1201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of these loops."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797410,
    "dDurationMs": 5289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that doesn't mean the loops are physical,\nthey're just a way to parameterize the quantum-scale"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802699,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "geometry of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 804939,
    "dDurationMs": 6250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Where we transition from the abstract to the\nphysical is not at all clear, and, honestly,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811189,
    "dDurationMs": 1471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hurts my head."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812660,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dig into spin networks and spin foam if you\nwant a headache too."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817809,
    "dDurationMs": 4351,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Serenity Receiver asked about the experiment to test Loop Quantum Gravity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822160,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So LQG predicts that light of different wavelengths travels at very slightly different speeds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828600,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this was NOT observed in the light from a\ndistant gamma ray burst, which presents a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834350,
    "dDurationMs": 2099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "challenge for the theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836449,
    "dDurationMs": 3571,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So why does LQG predict different speeds of\nlight?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840020,
    "dDurationMs": 4919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, if space is quantized on tiny scales, then\nwe expect the very shortest wavelengths of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844939,
    "dDurationMs": 6070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light to be slightly perturbed by these quantum\ncells of space - sort of like traveling through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851009,
    "dDurationMs": 4571,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cracked glass - they interact with the edges\nand slow down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855580,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wavelengths longer than this quantum scale\ncan ignore this fragmentation and so travel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861170,
    "dDurationMs": 1050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at normal speed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862220,
    "dDurationMs": 5119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There were some good questions on the Typler\ncylinder - that's the infintiely long cylinder"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867340,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that you can travel around to end up back\nwhere you started - in both time and space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872360,
    "dDurationMs": 7180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some One asks if a circle in 2 spatial dimensions\nwould allow for closed time like curves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879540,
    "dDurationMs": 1960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually yes!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881500,
    "dDurationMs": 2519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I saw this in at least one paper."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884019,
    "dDurationMs": 3310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Time travel is easy in flatland."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887329,
    "dDurationMs": 4971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Troy Henry asked if a torus would serve as\nan infinitely long cylinder"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892300,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- well the answer is, sadly, no."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894100,
    "dDurationMs": 3669,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The cylinder has to literally go on forever\nin both directions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 897769,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the beginning of the time travel episode\nI invited future time travelers to show up"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902429,
    "dDurationMs": 2571,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on set. None of you did."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905000,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But several of you got onto the comments to say\nI forgot to post the address of the studio one year from now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911120,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But I have to ask - why did you wait until after\nthat episode to tell me that?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916040,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Surely as time travelers you could have reminded\nme in the loop quantum gravity comments the week before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922000,
    "dDurationMs": 1260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I'm suspicious."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923260,
    "dDurationMs": 5739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or maybe Stephen Hawking's chronology projection\nconjecture prohibits anyone from remembering"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928999,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to post addresses to time traveler parties."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 932579,
    "dDurationMs": 6190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Guy Frost has a better explanation - YouTube\nwon't survive into the far future."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938769,
    "dDurationMs": 3611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I guess when it goes offline you can blame that\ninvitation."
    } ]
  } ]
}
